    "updated_at": "2019-12-28T16:26:02Z",
    "body": "Disclaimer: My personal opinion. Not claiming to represent anyone.\r\n\r\nIt's not hard to implement this feature request for Zsh but I doubt many users will want it. Virtually all themes, simple and complex, display `$?` as exit status. Thus, if `❯` in starship prompt is green, `echo $?` will print zero. If it's red, `echo $?` will print non-zero.\r\n\r\nBuiltin prompt expansion in zsh also behaves the same way. For example:\r\n\r\n```zsh\r\n❯ PROMPT='%(?.ok.error)> ' zsh -df\r\nok> false\r\nerror> true\r\nok> false\r\nerror> \r\nerror> \r\nerror>\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt's possible to change this behavior on the number of levels (although I wouldn't recommend it). For example, like this:\r\n\r\n```zsh\r\nfunction accept-line() {\r\n  [[ -n $BUFFER ]] || BUFFER=:\r\n  zle .accept-line\r\n}\r\nzle -N accept-line\r\n```\r\n\r\nOr like this:\r\n\r\n```zsh\r\nfunction accept-line() {\r\n  if [[ -n $BUFFER ]]; then\r\n    zle .accept-line\r\n  else\r\n    for f in precmd $precmd_functions; do\r\n      (( $+functions[$f] )) && $f &>/dev/null\r\n    done\r\n    zle .reset-prompt\r\n  fi\r\n}\r\nzle -N accept-line\r\n```",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
